description.narrative:
The programme is delivered through a phased approach:

Phase I: National policy planning and evidence generation - The programme supported five SIDS to establish plastic waste baselines, analyse future waste and leakage scenarios, and develop evidence-based national policy plans and recommendation reports. These outputs set out quantified pathways to reduce plastic pollution and strengthened government ownership through stakeholder engagement and policy co-development.

Extension phase: Implementation readiness and regional feasibility scoping - The programme supported the transition from planning to implementation readiness by:
1. completing financial assessments for Saint Lucia and Guinea-Bissau, including policy costings, financing pathways, and implementation sequencing;
2. embedding technical support to strengthen institutional capacity, governance arrangements, approval processes, and policy implementation planning;
3. advancing regional EPR work with the Indian Ocean Commission through stakeholder engagement and completion of a Scope of Work for a feasibility study; and
4. supporting regional dissemination and peer learning in the Pacific through the Tuvalu national launch and a Pacific SIDS peer exchange.

Phase III: Phased implementation-readiness support and early implementation - From April 2026 to March 2027, the programme will build on previous outputs by:
1. developing an implementation finance assessment for Papua New Guinea;
2. strengthening institutional capacity in Grenada or Tuvalu;
3. advancing early implementation in Saint Lucia through a reuse-focused pilot;
4. progressing regional EPR through feasibility follow-on work and funding mobilisation in the Indian Ocean region; and
5. engaging private sector actors to identify practical solutions and financing pathways relevant to SIDS.

This phased model enables countries to progress according to readiness, available funding, and government priorities, while maintaining momentum across the five partner countries.
